@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: Kant on Race -- Textual Evidence and the Race/Anthropology Corpus
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-27
PAPERS_FOUND: 17 total (High: 9, Medium: 6, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Kant's writings on race span four decades of his career and include three
dedicated essays ("Of the Different Human Races," 1775/1777; "Determination
of the Concept of a Human Race," 1785; "On the Use of Teleological
Principles in Philosophy," 1788), lecture notes on Physical Geography and
Anthropology, and scattered remarks throughout the critical corpus. Kant's
race theory is grounded in his account of natural teleology: races are
heritable modifications of an original human stock, triggered by climate and
environment, which then become fixed as permanent germs (Keime). This
monogenist framework leads Kant to rank the four races hierarchically, with
white Europeans at the summit and Africans and Native Americans portrayed as
deficient in the capacity for rational self-governance.

The scholarly literature divides sharply over whether this racism is
incidental or systemic. The "developmental" or recantation thesis
(Kleingeld 2007) argues Kant substantially revised his racial hierarchy in
the 1790s as he developed his cosmopolitan ethics. Against this, Bernasconi
(2002, 2011), Lu-Adler (2022, 2023), and Mills (2014) argue for continuity:
Kant's race theory persisted into his late work, and the cosmopolitan
writings do not retract the hierarchical claims. A third debate concerns
the relationship between the race theory and Kant's transcendental
philosophy: Sandford (2018), Marwah (2022), and Ferrini (2023) argue the
teleological framework of the third Critique gives race a systematic
philosophical role, while Mosayebi (2025) shows Kant's metaphysics of race
constitutes a distinctive position he calls "race teleo-rationalism."

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain provides the textual and philological basis for the entire
literature review: any assessment of Kant and colonialism must begin with
what Kant actually said about race and non-European peoples, in which
texts, and with what degree of philosophical systematicity. The
developmental vs. continuity debate directly bears on whether Kant's
cosmopolitanism can be rehabilitated or must be read as always already
racially structured.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The Physical Geography lectures remain understudied relative to the race
essays and Anthropology; only Lu-Adler (2023) and van Gorkom's forthcoming
work address the lectures directly. Eze's "Color of Reason" essay (1997)
-- an important early text from Anthropology and the Question of Race
(Battiste ed.) -- was identified in PhilPapers but could not be separately
verified via CrossRef as a standalone chapter; it appears in the Lu-Adler
monograph's bibliography. The Mikkelsen anthology (2013) provides primary
texts but was omitted as a secondary source for space reasons.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize around three axes: (1) the structure and sources of Kant's
race theory (teleology, epigenesis, natural history methodology),
(2) the developmental vs. continuity debate, and (3) the question of
systematic integration of racist claims into Kant's broader
philosophical architecture (moral theory, cosmopolitanism, pedagogy).
Pair the Kleingeld-Lu-Adler exchange (2007/2022/2024/2025) as the
central debate node.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Continuity/critical: 7 papers -- Kant's racism is systematic and
  persists across his career
- Developmental/recantation: 2 papers -- Kant revised racial hierarchy
  by the 1790s
- Philosophical anatomy: 5 papers -- Focus on teleological, metaphysical,
  or methodological structure of race theory
- Contextualizing/mitigating: 3 papers -- non-racist or nuanced readings
====================================================================
}

@book{luadler2023kant,
  author    = {Lu-Adler, Huaping},
  title     = {Kant, Race, and Racism: Views from Somewhere},
  year      = {2023},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  address   = {New York},
  doi       = {10.1093/oso/9780197685211.001.0001},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lu-Adler argues that Kant's racism was not a set of peripheral
  prejudices inconsistent with his philosophy but an ideological formation he
  actively constructed through his dual role as Naturforscher (natural philosopher
  of race) and educator. Drawing on archival work including lecture notes and
  pedagogical writings, she demonstrates that Kant used his academic platform to
  instil a racial worldview in which European white men were uniquely positioned
  to realize human moral progress. The book traces the development of Kant's race
  theory from Baconian natural history through its racialization of human difference
  and into his pedagogical influence.

  RELEVANCE: The most recent comprehensive scholarly treatment of Kant and race,
  immediately the central reference point in the field. Directly relevant to the
  project's concern with how Kant's colonial attitudes were embedded in his
  philosophical system, not merely accidental to it, and to the question of
  whether Kantian universalism can be retrieved for anti-colonial purposes.

  POSITION: Continuity/critical: Kant's racism is pervasive and systematic,
  constitutive of his role as public intellectual and educator.
  },
  keywords  = {Kant, race, racism, anthropology, natural-history, colonialism, High},
  abstract = {Abstract Kant scholars have paid relatively little attention to his raciology. They assume that his racism, as personal prejudice, can be disentangled from his core philosophy. They also assume that racism contradicts his moral theory. This book challenges both assumptions. It shows how Kant's raciology---divided into racialism and racism---is integral to his philosophical system as a whole. It also rejects the individualistic approach to Kant and racism. Instead, it uses the notion of racism as ideological formation to demonstrate how Kant, from his social location both as a prominent scholar and as a lifelong educator, participated in the formation of modern racist ideology. As a scholar, Kant developed a groundbreaking scientific theory of race from the standpoint of a philosophical investigator of nature or Naturforscher. As an educator, he transmitted denigrating depictions of the racialized others and imbued them with normative relevance. In both roles, he presented a worldview that excluded nonwhites from such goods as recognitional respect and candidacy for cultural and moral achievements.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{kleingeld2007second,
  author    = {Kleingeld, Pauline},
  title     = {{KANT'S SECOND THOUGHTS ON RACE}},
  journal   = {The Philosophical Quarterly},
  year      = {2007},
  volume    = {57},
  number    = {229},
  pages     = {573--592},
  doi       = {10.1111/j.1467-9213.2007.498.x},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kleingeld argues that Kant's position on race was not stable
  across his career. While his published work in the 1780s defended white
  superiority over non-whites as part of his race theory, in the 1790s he
  progressively restricted the concept of race to natural science, dropped his
  hierarchical account of racial capacities, and moved toward a genuinely
  egalitarian and cosmopolitan view. The article carefully distinguishes between
  Kant's scientific race theory and his normative rankings, arguing that he
  retracted the latter.

  RELEVANCE: This article launched the dominant interpretive debate in the
  literature -- the "recantation thesis" -- and is indispensable for the project's
  treatment of whether Kant's later cosmopolitanism can be deployed against
  colonialism. It establishes the diachronic framework for reading Kant on race
  that subsequent scholars (Bernasconi, Lu-Adler, Mills, Smith) explicitly contest.

  POSITION: Developmental/recantation: Kant genuinely changed his mind about
  racial hierarchy in the 1790s in favour of moral egalitarianism.
  },
  keywords  = {Kant, race, recantation, cosmopolitanism, Kleingeld, High},
  abstract = {During the 1780s, as Kant was developing his universalistic moral theory, he published texts in which he defended the superiority of whites over non-whites. Whether commentators see this as evidence of inconsistent universalism or of consistent inegalitarianism, they generally assume that Kant's position on race remained stable during the 1780s and 1790s. Against this standard view, I argue on the basis of his texts that Kant radically changed his mind. I examine his 1780s race theory and his hierarchical conception of the races, and subsequently address the question of the significance of these views, especially in the light of Kant's own ethical theory. I then show that during the 1790s Kant restricts the role of the concept of race, and drops his hierarchical account of the races in favour of a more genuinely egalitarian and cosmopolitan view.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{allais2016racism,
  author    = {Allais, Lucy},
  title     = {Kant's Racism},
  journal   = {Philosophical Papers},
  year      = {2016},
  volume    = {45},
  number    = {1-2},
  pages     = {1--36},
  doi       = {10.1080/05568641.2016.1199170},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Against Mills's thesis that Kant's racism is best understood
  as a threshold notion of moral personhood (whereby some races are excluded
  from moral consideration), Allais argues that Kant cannot be made internally
  consistent on race at all. Rather than forcing consistency, she proposes that
  Kant's racism should be read as a philosophically illuminating case study of
  what racism is: the persistence of false, entrenched beliefs about groups
  that resist rational correction even when the agent holds universalist moral
  commitments.

  RELEVANCE: The article intervenes in the debate over how Kant's racism relates
  to his moral theory, with direct bearing on whether Kantian ethics can be
  "salvaged" for anti-colonial purposes. Its conceptual account of racism as
  ideologically entrenched false belief rather than formal exclusion from moral
  standing has implications for how we assess the relationship between Kant's
  race texts and his critical philosophy.

  POSITION: Continuity/critical: Kant's racism is incompatible with his
  universalism, but this reveals something about the nature of racism rather
  than a route to consistency.
  },
  keywords  = {Kant, racism, moral-theory, Mills, universalism, High},
  abstract = {After a long period of comparative neglect, in the last few decades growing numbers of philosophers have been paying attention to the startling contrast presented between Kant's universal moral theory, with its inspiring enlightenment ideas of human autonomy, equality and dignity and Kant's racism. Against Charles Mills, who argues that the way to make Kant consistent is by attributing to him a threshold notion of moral personhood, according to which some races do not qualify for consideration under the categorical imperative, I argue that Kant cannot be made consistent on race, and that rather than trying to make him so, we should use the example of Kant's racism to tell us something about the nature of racism. I argue that Kant's own moral philosophy and moral psychology in fact give some materials for thinking about his racism, and about racism.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{marwah2022white,
  author    = {Marwah, Inder S.},
  title     = {White Progress: {Kant}, Race and Teleology},
  journal   = {Kantian Review},
  year      = {2022},
  volume    = {27},
  number    = {4},
  pages     = {615--634},
  doi       = {10.1017/S1369415422000334},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Marwah argues that Kant's racial theory is only fully
  intelligible within his teleological natural history, and that this framework
  structurally constrains non-white peoples' capacity for moral progress. The
  teleological embedding of race means that even if Kant's political theory or
  late cosmopolitanism appear more egalitarian, the underlying natural-historical
  framework still assigns differential moral-developmental capacities along
  racial lines.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the intersection of Kant's teleology (central
  to his third Critique) with race, showing that the philosophical architecture
  of the critical system itself encodes racial hierarchy. This is crucial for
  the project's concern with how colonialism is implicated in Kant's systematic
  philosophy, not just his empirical writings.

  POSITION: Continuity/critical: teleological natural history entrenches
  racial hierarchy in the philosophical system as a whole.
  },
  keywords  = {Kant, teleology, race, natural-history, progress, High},
  abstract = {Abstract This article examines how Kant's conceptualizations of natural history and teleological judgement shape his understanding of human difference and race. I argue that the teleological framework encasing Kant's racial theory implies constraints on the capacity of non-whites to make moral progress. While commentators tend to approach Kant's racial theory in relation to his political theory, his late-life cosmopolitanism, and his treatments (or non-treatments) of colonialism, empire and slavery, the problem I focus on here is that race is itself only intelligible in relation to a teleological natural history limiting certain races' capacities to engage in humanity's moral vocation.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{luadler2022travel,
  author    = {Lu-Adler, Huaping},
  title     = {Kant's Use of Travel Reports in Theorizing about Race: A Case Study of How Testimony Features in Natural Philosophy},
  journal   = {Studies in History and Philosophy of Science},
  year      = {2022},
  volume    = {91},
  pages     = {10--19},
  doi       = {10.1016/j.shpsa.2021.10.020},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lu-Adler examines Kant's methodology in the three race essays,
  showing that he operated as a Naturforscher (philosophical investigator of
  nature) who used travel reports as a form of testimony to be selected,
  interpreted, and organized by philosophical reason. This rationalist theory
  of testimony explains both Kant's selective use of travelers' accounts and
  his confidence in racially hierarchical empirical generalizations he never
  directly observed.

  RELEVANCE: Clarifies how Kant's race theory relates to his broader epistemology
  of natural philosophy, showing that the methodology of the race essays is
  continuous with (not deviant from) his philosophical practice. Relevant to
  the project's interest in how philosophical method enabled rather than
  constrained colonial epistemologies.

  POSITION: Philosophical anatomy: methodology of Kant's race theory
  within his broader natural philosophy.
  },
  keywords  = {Kant, race, natural-philosophy, testimony, travel-reports, High},
  abstract = {A testimony is somebody else's reported experience of what has happened. It is an indispensable source of knowledge. It only gives us historical cognition, however, which stands in a complex relation to rational or philosophical cognition: while the latter presupposes historical cognition as its matter, one needs the architectonic "eye of a philosopher" to select, interpret, and organize historical cognition. Kant develops this rationalist theory of testimony. He also practices it in his own work, especially while theorizing about race as a subject of natural philosophy. In three dedicated essays on this subject, he treats race from the standpoint of a philosophical investigator of nature (Naturforscher), who (as Kant puts it in the first Critique) learns from nature "like an appointed judge who compels witnesses to answer the questions he puts to them." This view underwrites Kant's use of travel reports (a type of testimony) in developing and defending his theory of race.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{luadler2022slavery,
  author    = {Lu-Adler, Huaping},
  title     = {Kant and Slavery -- Or Why He Never Became a Racial Egalitarian},
  journal   = {Critical Philosophy of Race},
  year      = {2022},
  volume    = {10},
  number    = {2},
  pages     = {263--294},
  doi       = {10.5325/critphilrace.10.2.0263},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lu-Adler challenges the view that Kant's late-career criticisms
  of certain forms of slavery constitute evidence of racial egalitarianism. She
  argues that Kant's belated condemnations of colonial slavery were framed in
  terms of rights of trade and contract rather than racial equality, and that
  his racial hierarchy remained structurally intact. The article distinguishes
  Kant's gradual moral evolution on specific practices from any repudiation of
  the racial framework underpinning those practices.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most direct engagement with the intersection of Kant's
  views on slavery, race, and colonialism, arguing against Kleingeld's recantation
  thesis specifically in the domain of colonial slavery. Essential for the
  project's assessment of whether Kant's critical philosophy of right can ground
  anti-colonial conclusions.

  POSITION: Continuity/critical: Kant never abandoned racial hierarchy
  even in repudiating some colonial practices.
  },
  keywords  = {Kant, slavery, race, colonialism, High},
  abstract = {Abstract:According to an oft-repeated narrative, while Kant maintained racist views through the 1780s, he changed his mind in the 1790s. Pauline Kleingeld introduced this narrative based on passages from Kant's Metaphysics of Morals (1797) and "Toward Perpetual Peace" (1795). On her reading, Kant categorically condemned chattel slavery (and colonialism) in those texts, which meant that he became more racially egalitarian. But the passages involving slavery, once contextualized, either do not concern modern, race-based chattel slavery or at best suggest that Kant mentioned it as a cautionary tale for labor practices in Europe. Overall, Kant never explicitly considered chattel slavery as a moral problem to be addressed on its own. Rather, he treated it primarily in terms of its function in human history. If he ended up expressing some qualms about its practices, it was likely because they threatened to deepen intra-European conflicts and undermine the prospect of perpetual peace. The humanity of the enslaved "Negroes" was never part of the reasoning. This was not a casual oversight on Kant's part. It reflects the complexity of his philosophical system: everything he did or did not say about chattel slavery begins to make sense once we connect his philosophy of history and his depiction of "Negroes" as natural slaves.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{mills2014redux,
  author    = {Mills, Charles W.},
  title     = {Kant and Race, Redux},
  journal   = {Graduate Faculty Philosophy Journal},
  year      = {2014},
  volume    = {35},
  number    = {1},
  pages     = {125--157},
  doi       = {10.5840/gfpj2014351/27},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mills revisits his earlier arguments about Kant's racial
  subpersonhood thesis in response to Kleingeld's developmental challenge.
  He maintains that Kant's anthropological writings imply a hierarchical account
  of personhood under which Africans and others are not full moral persons
  deserving equal treatment under the categorical imperative. Mills defends
  reading Kant as a "sub-person theorist" and argues that Kleingeld's
  developmental account underestimates the extent and philosophical depth of
  Kant's racism.

  RELEVANCE: Mills is the key defender of the view that Kant's racism
  contaminates his moral theory at the level of its application -- that the
  categorical imperative was not intended to apply equally across races. This
  is essential for debates about whether Kant's ethics can be used to ground
  anti-colonial critique or whether it is structurally compromised.

  POSITION: Continuity/subpersonhood: Kant's racism implies racially
  differentiated moral personhood.
  },
  keywords  = {Kant, race, subpersonhood, categorical-imperative, continuity-thesis, High},
  abstract = {The article is a contribution to the literature on Kant's racism. It revisits and expands on Mills's earlier claims about Kant's "Untermenschen" (subpersons), in which non-white races are, to varying degrees, excluded from full moral consideration under the categorical imperative. The paper responds in detail to Kleingeld's recent "recantation" thesis about Kant's alleged change of mind on race in the 1790s, contending that her argument fails to establish the crucial distinction between Kant's race science and his racial hierarchy of moral persons.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{mosayebi2025metaphysics,
  author    = {Mosayebi, Reza},
  title     = {Kant's Metaphysics of Race, Its Distinctiveness, and Its Normativity},
  journal   = {Journal of Social Philosophy},
  year      = {2025},
  doi       = {10.1111/josp.12612},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Using the contemporary taxonomy of the metaphysics of race,
  Mosayebi argues that Kant's race theory occupies a distinctive metaphysical
  position he calls "race teleo-rationalism": unlike race essentialism or race
  naturalism, Kant's position grounds racial hierarchy in a priori teleological
  principles of reason rather than purely empirical facts. He argues that this
  metaphysical distinctiveness makes Kant's racism particularly significant: it
  shows that racism can be licensed by rational a priori principles.

  RELEVANCE: Connects Kant's race theory to contemporary metaphysics of race
  debates, clarifying what is philosophically novel about Kant's position. The
  claim that rational a priori principles can generate racism is directly
  relevant to assessing whether Kantian universalism provides any check against
  colonial ideology.

  POSITION: Philosophical anatomy: Kant's race theory constitutes "race
  teleo-rationalism," a distinct metaphysical position grounded in a priori
  teleological reason.
  },
  keywords  = {Kant, metaphysics-of-race, teleology, normativity, High},
  abstract = {Drawing on the contemporary taxonomy of the metaphysics of race, this paper shows that Kant's theory of race occupies a distinct metaphysical position on race. Second, it argues that Kant's metaphysics of race inherently produces racist claims. Contrasting Kant's race theory with other metaphysical positions on race allows us to both understand his theory and its racist aspects better, and to improve the map of metaphysical positions on race. Closer analysis of Kant's metaphysics of race reveals that, contrary to common belief, it is not race essentialism (traditional racialism), it is inaccurate to see it just as a variant of race naturalism, and both racial development and racial hierarchy in Kant's theory are more complex than they seem on most scholars' reading of him. Kant's metaphysics of race, which will be called race teleo-rationalism, shows that racism could be justified by "reason" and its a priori principles and assumptions.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

