  that even Kant's last dedicated race writing does not retract his hierarchical
  views. The connection to physical geography is also directly relevant to the
  colonialism project.

  POSITION: Continuity: the 1788 essay deepens rather than revises Kant's
  racial hierarchy.
  },
  keywords = {Kant, race-essays, teleology, physical-geography, continuity-thesis, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract},
}

@misc{bernasconi2002unfamiliar,
  author       = {Bernasconi, Robert},
  title        = {Kant as an Unfamiliar Source of Racism},
  year         = {2002},
  howpublished = {In Julie Ward and Tommy Lott (eds.), \textit{Philosophers on Race: Critical Essays}. Oxford: Blackwell, pp.\ 145--166},
  doi          = {10.1002/9780470753514.ch8},
  note         = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Bernasconi argues that Kant should be recognized as a major,
  though often overlooked, source of modern scientific racism. He traces how
  Kant's systematic theory of race -- his first in 1775 -- gave race a
  philosophical foundation it had previously lacked and shows that Kant's
  biological monogenism was paired with a normative hierarchism that ranked
  races in terms of capacity for civilization and moral development.

  RELEVANCE: This essay provides the historical and conceptual case for treating
  Kant as not merely a participant in existing racial discourse but as an active
  constructor of the modern scientific concept of race. It is foundational for
  the colonialism project's argument about Enlightenment philosophy's complicity
  with colonial racial ideology.

  POSITION: Continuity/constructivist: Kant helped invent the modern
  philosophical concept of race.
  },
  keywords = {Kant, race, Enlightenment, scientific-racism, history-of-race, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract},
}

@article{sandford2018naturalhistory,
  author    = {Sandford, Stella},
  title     = {Kant, Race, and Natural History},
  journal   = {Philosophy \& Social Criticism},
  year      = {2018},
  volume    = {44},
  number    = {9},
  pages     = {950--977},
  doi       = {10.1177/0191453718768358},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Sandford situates Kant's race theory within the broader
  tradition of eighteenth-century natural history, examining how his account
  of human races relates to his distinction between natural description
  (Naturbeschreibung) and natural history (Naturgeschichte) proper. She argues
  that Kant's insistence on race as a genuine natural-historical concept is part
  of his attempt to give teleological natural history scientific status, and that
  this scientific ambition is inseparable from the racial hierarchy the theory
  generates. She also traces how this problem feeds into the first and third
  Critiques.

  RELEVANCE: Provides intellectual-historical contextualization of Kant's race
  theory within the history of natural science, while demonstrating connections
  to the critical philosophy. Relevant to the project's concern with how
  philosophical and scientific frameworks enabled colonial epistemologies.

  POSITION: Philosophical anatomy: race theory as part of Kant's project
  for natural history as a scientific discipline, with implications for
  the critical system.
  },
  keywords  = {Kant, race, natural-history, natural-science, critique-of-judgment, Medium},
  abstract = {This article presents a new argument concerning the relation between Kant's theory of race and aspects of the critical philosophy. It argues that Kant's treatment of the problem of the systematic unity of nature and knowledge in the Critique of Pure Reason and the Critique of the Power of Judgment can be traced back a methodological problem in the natural history of the period. Kant's transformation of the methodological problem from natural history into a set of philosophical (and specifically epistemological) problems proceeds by way of the working out of his own problem in natural history -- the problem of the natural history of the human races -- and specifically the problem of the unity in diversity of the human species, in response to which he develops a theory of race. This theory of race is, further, the first developed model of the use of teleological judgment in Kant's work. The article thus argues that Kant's philosophical position on the systematic unity of nature and of knowledge in the first and third Critiques, and his account and defense of teleological judgment, are developed out of problems first articulated in his solution to the problem of the unity in diversity of the human species.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{ferrini2023epigenesis,
  author    = {Ferrini, Cinzia},
  title     = {The Epigenesis of Germs and Dispositions in Logic and Life: {Kant}'s System of Pure Reason and His Concept of Race},
  journal   = {SATS},
  year      = {2023},
  volume    = {24},
  number    = {2},
  pages     = {111--128},
  doi       = {10.1515/sats-2023-0013},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ferrini examines the philosophical connection between Kant's
  adoption of epigenesis as a model for his system of pure reason (in the
  Transcendental Deduction, B167) and his theory of race in the 1775/1777
  essays. She argues that the Kantian race theory provides the background
  context for assessing Blumenbach's later shift to epigenesis, and that the
  structural analogy between germs/predispositions in race and the a priori
  concepts in the first Critique is philosophically significant rather than
  merely metaphorical.

  RELEVANCE: One of the few studies that connects Kant's race theory to the
  first Critique itself, arguing that the same conceptual apparatus (germs,
  predispositions, epigenesis) functions in both natural philosophy and
  transcendental idealism. Relevant to assessing how deeply Kant's racial
  concepts penetrate his critical system.

  POSITION: Philosophical anatomy: structural connection between race
  theory and transcendental philosophy via the epigenesis model.
  },
  keywords  = {Kant, epigenesis, race, Critique-of-Pure-Reason, germs, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract In the 1787 Transcendental Deduction of the Categories Kant indicates the only possible ways by which one can account for a necessary agreement of experience with the concepts of its objects (B166), using analogies between modes of explanation and biological theories about the origin of life. He endorses epigenesis as a model for his system of pure reason (B167). This paper examines various interpretive claims about the meaning of this theory of generation and its significance for Kant's philosophy (Section 1), showing that, after his Critical shift in perspective, in 1775/77 Kant already combined preformed elements and their purposively oriented formation by natural forces (Section 2). Contrary to the standard view, Kant's theory of race appears to constitute the background to assess Blumenbach's later (1799/1781) shift to epigenesis after supporting Haller's preformism (Section 3). In Section 4, I argue that the ground of affinity between epigenesis and formal idealism rests in tracing the first origin of these conformities: external a posteriori climate conditions and predisposed germs and dispositions within the generative power of the human body; and external a posteriori experience and spontaneous a priori concepts of its objects, within pure sensory intuiting and pure thinking.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{cohen2006epigenesis,
  author    = {Cohen, Alix A.},
  title     = {Kant on Epigenesis, Monogenesis and Human Nature: The Biological Premises of Anthropology},
  journal   = {Studies in History and Philosophy of Science Part C: Studies in History and Philosophy of Biological and Biomedical Sciences},
  year      = {2006},
  volume    = {37},
  number    = {4},
  pages     = {675--693},
  doi       = {10.1016/j.shpsc.2006.09.005},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Cohen reconstructs the biological foundations of Kant's
  anthropology, focusing on his commitments to epigenesis and monogenesis (the
  common origin of all human races). She shows how these biological premises
  underpin Kant's account of race as fixed hereditary modifications of a common
  human stock, triggered by climate, and why Kant's biological framework implies
  the permanent heritability of racial characteristics.

  RELEVANCE: Understanding the biological premises of Kant's race theory is
  essential for assessing its internal coherence and its relationship to
  contemporary biology. Cohen's paper provides the background for understanding
  why Kant saw race as permanent and heritable, which in turn explains the
  strength of his racial hierarchism.

  POSITION: Philosophical anatomy/philological: reconstructs biological
  foundations of Kantian race theory.
  },
  keywords  = {Kant, epigenesis, monogenesis, biology, anthropology, Medium},
  abstract = {This paper examines Kant's views on epigenesis, monogenesis and human nature in the context of his anthropological writings. It argues that Kant's commitment to monogenesis (the view that all human beings descend from a single common ancestor) and to epigenesis (the view that organisms are not preformed but develop through an active formative power) are interconnected and play a crucial role in his account of human nature, and in particular in his theory of race.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{hedrick2008race,
  author    = {Hedrick, Todd},
  title     = {Race, Difference, and Anthropology in {Kant}'s Cosmopolitanism},
  journal   = {Journal of the History of Philosophy},
  year      = {2008},
  volume    = {46},
  number    = {2},
  pages     = {245--268},
  doi       = {10.1353/hph.0.0001},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Hedrick examines how the Anthropology from a Pragmatic Point
  of View relates to Kant's cosmopolitan political theory, arguing that the
  empirical anthropological observations on racial and cultural difference create
  a tension with the formal universalism of his cosmopolitanism. He traces how
  Kant regards national-cultural differences rooted in racial biology as
  essential for the cosmopolitan union, and argues that this creates an
  irresolvable difficulty for Kant's account of moral progress.

  RELEVANCE: Bridges two key domains -- the anthropology corpus and Kant's
  cosmopolitanism -- that are both central to the project. Helps understand
  how Kant's empirical claims about races were integrated into or bracketed
  from his normative political theory.

  POSITION: Philosophical anatomy: tension between empirical anthropological
  content and formal cosmopolitan universalism.
  },
  keywords  = {Kant, anthropology, cosmopolitanism, race, difference, Medium},
  abstract = {This paper explores the connections between Kant's theory of hierarchical racial difference, on the one hand, and his cosmopolitanism and conceptions of moral and political progress, on the other. I argue that Kant's racial biology plays an essential role in maintaining national-cultural differences, which he views as essential for the establishment of the cosmopolitan union. Unfortunately, not only are these views racist, they also complicate Kant's ability to consistently think through the prospect of the human species' moral progress.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{mosayebi2025racemixing,
  author    = {Mosayebi, Reza},
  title     = {Kant on Race Mixing, Subracial Variety, and Progressive Culture},
  journal   = {Critical Philosophy of Race},
  year      = {2025},
  volume    = {13},
  number    = {1},
  pages     = {53--74},
  doi       = {10.5325/critphilrace.13.1.0053},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mosayebi draws attention to the underexplored category of
  "variety" (Varietaet) in Kant's race theory -- a sub-racial level restricted
  to the white race that provides the internal diversity necessary for cultural
  progress. He argues that Kant's opposition to race mixing is not merely a
  biologistic prejudice but is integral to his teleological account of cultural
  progress, which assigns the generative role in civilizational development
  exclusively to white sub-racial variety.

  RELEVANCE: Reveals a structural connection between Kant's race theory and his
  philosophy of culture and progress that has not been previously analyzed.
  Directly bears on the project's concern with how Kant's racial hierarchy is
  embedded in his philosophy of history and teleology.

  POSITION: Philosophical anatomy: structural role of race and variety
  in Kant's teleological account of cultural progress.
  },
  keywords  = {Kant, race-mixing, variety, culture, teleology, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract:Critical scholarship of Kant's race theory has been mainly focused on his concept of race. This article draws attention to his peculiar conception of "variety" as a subracial category that, restricted to the White race, plays a significant role in providing a diversity that enables progress in culture. The question organizing the article is this: How, on Kant's account, given its defense of radical racial inequality and degradation by race mixing, can the human diversity needed for substantial cultural achievements come about? Analyzing Kant's opposition to race mixing, this article shows that its corollary is a teleological conception of the diversity within the White racial group (White diversity).},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{stubenrauchmateo2024teleology,
  author    = {Mart{\'i}nez Mateo, Marina and Stubenrauch, Heiko},
  title     = {Teleological Judgment between Racism and Its Critique: On the Status of Race in {Kant}'s Work},
  journal   = {Rivista di Estetica},
  year      = {2024},
  volume    = {87},
  pages     = {81--98},
  doi       = {10.4000/13tuo},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: The authors argue that Kant's engagement with race can only
  be understood within his teleological theory, in which nature is interpreted
  as a purposive whole directed toward the realization of freedom. While
  acknowledging that teleology thus makes race a philosophically meaningful
  concept that extends beyond natural philosophy to deny non-white people the
  ability to participate in the actualization of reason, they argue for an
  alternative form of teleological judgment that can serve as a resource for
  criticizing rather than legitimizing racism.

  RELEVANCE: Examines the dual potential of Kant's teleological framework --
  as both the ground of racial hierarchy and a possible resource for critique --
  relevant to the project's interest in whether German Idealist resources can
  be retrieved for anti-colonial purposes.

  POSITION: Philosophical anatomy with reconstructive intent: teleology grounds
  race hierarchy but may be reconstructed to critique racism.
  },
  keywords  = {Kant, teleology, race, critique, reconstruction, Low},
  abstract = {Over the last three decades, considerable debate has surrounded Immanuel Kant's views on race. In this article, we propose to address the question of race philosophically, explicitly and systematically relating it to Kant's critical work. We show that Kant's engagement with race can only be fully comprehended within the context of his teleological theory, wherein he interprets nature as a purposive whole facilitating the realization of freedom. Since Kant understands this teleological theory as the completion of his critical work, it occupies a key position in his philosophy, turning race into a philosophically meaningful concept. We conclude by advocating for an alternative form of teleological judgment that can serve as a means to criticize racism, rather than legitimize it.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{luadler2023geography,
  author    = {Lu-Adler, Huaping},
  title     = {Know Your Place, Know Your Calling: Geography, Race, and {Kant}'s ``{World-Citizen}''},
  journal   = {Studia Kantiana},
  year      = {2023},
  doi       = {10.5380/sk.v21i2.92101},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lu-Adler traces Kant's use of his geography and anthropology
  courses to form students -- Occidental white men -- in the self-perception
  that they were naturally positioned by their geographic location on Earth to
  be uniquely obliged and entitled to act as agents of human progress. She shows
  that this pedagogical function was integral to Kant's vision of world-citizenship,
  tracing the connection through works from the 1750s to his mature theory of
  race, moral theory, pragmatic anthropology, and theory of education.

  RELEVANCE: Directly connects the Physical Geography corpus to Kant's political
  philosophy and educational writings, showing how race and geography were
  integrated into the formation of a colonial world-civic identity. Essential
  context for the project's domain on Kant's cosmopolitanism and colonialism.

  POSITION: Continuity/critical: race and geography are constitutive
  of Kant's practical project of world-citizenship formation.
  },
  keywords  = {Kant, geography, race, world-citizen, cosmopolitanism, education, Low},
  abstract = {Anthropology and physical geography were among Kant's most popular and longest running courses. He intended them to give his students the world-knowledge that they needed in order to be effective world-citizens. Much of this indoctrination amounted to teaching Occidental white men, Kant's default audience, to perceive themselves as uniquely entitled and obliged to work as agents of human progress on the assumption that they, thanks to their geographic location on Earth, were naturally formed as an exceptional race.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}
